    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d358">

                <pb facs="0491" n="483" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg483"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/2344<hi rendition="#superscript">23/25</hi>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hyde Park</hi>, N. Y.</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-09-28">September 28,
                        1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I wholly agree with your pencilled note—to recite the more liberal original
                    attitude of the Japanese when they first sought the meeting, point out their
                    much narrowed position now, earnestly ask if they cannot go back to their
                    original attitude, start discussions again on agreement in principle, and
                    reemphasize my hope for a meeting.<anchor n="74" xml:id="d358-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D. R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d358-f1" n="74" xml:id="d358-f1-content">
                <p>See oral statement of October 2, <ref target="frus1931-41v02#pg_656">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 656</ref>.</p>
            </note>
